<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> go skiing in Japan from late December through February, with January usually the deepest month for Japan's famous light powder ("JAPOW"). Hokkaido (Niseko, Rusutsu, Kiroro, Furano) is the classic powder trip through New Chitose Airport near Sapporo. Honshu (Hakuba, Nozawa Onsen, Myoko) is reached by bullet train from Tokyo and adds bigger mountains and traditional villages. The Ikon Pass covers Niseko, Furano, Myoko Suginohara, and APPI; the Epic Pass covers Rusutsu and Hakuba Valley.</p></div>

<h2>When to go skiing in Japan</h2>
<p>Japan's snow comes from cold air crossing the Sea of Japan, which picks up moisture and drops it as frequent, light snowfall on the mountains. That pattern is strongest in mid-winter.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>December:</strong> resorts open and the base builds. Early December can be thin; late December is usually solid.</li>
  <li><strong>January:</strong> typically the snowiest month and the most reliable for deep powder days. It is also the busiest.</li>
  <li><strong>February:</strong> still excellent snow, with slightly more clear days. Lunar New Year and Japanese school holidays can bring crowds.</li>
  <li><strong>March:</strong> longer days and spring conditions, with fewer powder days but quieter slopes.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each resort's "best time to ski" page shows its own monthly snow pattern from 10 seasons of weather data, for example <%= link_to "best time to ski Niseko", best_time_resort_path("niseko-united") %> and <%= link_to "best time to ski Hakuba", best_time_resort_path("hakuba-valley") %>.</p>

