<h3>Hokkaido resorts</h3>
<ul>
  <li><%= link_to "Niseko United", resort_path("niseko-united") %>: Japan's best-known ski area, four linked resorts on one mountain with an international village and lots of English support.</li>
  <li><%= link_to "Rusutsu", resort_path("rusutsu") %>: a large resort across several peaks, known for tree runs, a short drive from Niseko.</li>
  <li><%= link_to "Kiroro", resort_path("kiroro") %>: a smaller resort that tends to catch a lot of snow, close to Sapporo and Otaru.</li>
  <li><%= link_to "Furano", resort_path("furano") %>: inland and colder, with dry snow and a working town at the base.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Honshu resorts</h3>
<ul>
  <li><%= link_to "Hakuba Valley", resort_path("hakuba-valley") %>: a valley of linked and nearby resorts in the Japanese Alps, home of the 1998 Winter Olympics alpine events. Snow Way's forecast is set at Happo-one.</li>
  <li><%= link_to "Nozawa Onsen", resort_path("nozawa-onsen") %>: a traditional hot spring village with the ski area rising right above it.</li>
  <li><%= link_to "Myoko Suginohara", resort_path("myoko-suginohara") %> and <%= link_to "Akakura Kanko", resort_path("akakura-kanko") %>: part of the Myoko area in Niigata, known for heavy snowfall.</li>
  <li><%= link_to "APPI Resort", resort_path("appi") %>: a big, well-groomed resort in Iwate, farther north on Honshu.</li>
</ul>
<p>See every Japan resort ranked by this week's forecast on the <%= link_to "Japan snow report", region_path("japan") %>.</p>

