<h2>Getting there</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Hokkaido:</strong> fly into New Chitose Airport (CTS), usually with a connection in Tokyo or another Asian hub. Airport buses and private transfers run to the main resorts, and roads are well maintained, though winter driving takes care.</li>
  <li><strong>Nagano and Niigata:</strong> fly into Tokyo Haneda (HND) or Narita, then take the Hokuriku bullet train (shinkansen) toward Nagano or Joetsu-Myoko and finish by bus or taxi.</li>
  <li><strong>Luggage:</strong> many travelers send ski bags ahead with a luggage delivery service rather than carry them on trains.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Tree skiing and out-of-bounds rules</h2>
<p>Japan's tree skiing is a big part of the appeal, but the rules matter. Many resorts allow access to off-piste and backcountry terrain only through designated gates, and some areas are closed entirely. Ducking ropes can mean losing your lift ticket, and rescue costs may be charged to you.</p>
<p>Terrain beyond resort gates is uncontrolled avalanche terrain, even when it's just past the lifts. Treat it like any backcountry trip: check the local avalanche information, travel with trained partners, and carry a beacon, probe, and shovel. Read <%= link_to "avalanche safety gear explained", guide_path("avalanche-safety-gear") %> and <%= link_to "backcountry skiing for beginners", guide_path("backcountry-skiing") %>, and consider a local guide for your first days in the trees.</p>

<h2>Onsen and etiquette basics</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Onsen (hot springs):</strong> wash and rinse thoroughly before getting in, keep towels out of the water, and expect to bathe without swimwear in separate men's and women's baths. Some onsen restrict visible tattoos; check ahead.</li>
  <li><strong>Lift lines:</strong> queues are orderly. Wait your turn and follow staff directions.</li>
  <li><strong>Shoes:</strong> take shoes off where you see a step up or shoe racks, including many lodges and restaurants.</li>
  <li><strong>Cash:</strong> card acceptance has grown, but carry some yen for small restaurants and village shops.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How long to stay</h2>
<p>Because the trip is long from North America, most skiers plan at least a week on snow. A common approach is 5 to 7 ski days at one base, or splitting time between two areas, such as Niseko and Rusutsu in Hokkaido, or Hakuba and Nozawa Onsen on Honshu. Build in a rest day: jet lag plus deep snow is tiring. Adding a few days in Tokyo or Sapporo is easy on the way in or out.</p>
<p>For trip logistics in general, see <%= link_to "how to plan a ski trip", guide_path("how-to-plan-a-ski-trip") %> and the <%= link_to "ski trip packing list", guide_path("ski-trip-packing-list") %>. For deep-snow technique, see <%= link_to "how to ski powder", guide_path("how-to-ski-powder") %>.</p>

